# Project file structure

Three concerns, three file kinds. Mixing them is a review blocker.

| File | Owns |
|---|---|
| `*.tsx` | Markup, composition, wiring — **no** business rules, **no** style declarations |
| `styled.ts` | All `StyleSheet.create` / styled-components declarations and stable style objects |
| `<name>Rules.ts` | Pure decisions, with a colocated `*.test.ts` |

## Top-level layout

```
src/
├── components/     # Reusable UI — design-system packages live here
├── screens/        # Screen components (grouped by auth state or domain)
├── stores/         # Zustand stores by feature domain
├── features/       # Pure feature logic (rules modules)
├── mappers/        # API request/response mapping
├── hooks/          # Custom React hooks
├── navigation/     # Navigators, route params, linking config
├── lib/            # Core services (HTTP client, WebSocket client, storage)
├── theme/          # Design tokens (colors, typography, measures)
├── i18n/           # Locales and i18n setup
└── utils/          # Cross-cutting pure helpers
```

## styled.ts rules

- **Never** declare styles inside `*.tsx` — including inline `style={{ … }}` objects that
  should be stable declarations.
- Colors and spacing are token-driven — no hard-coded hex or pixel values in `styled.ts`
  either (see `design-system/02-package-conventions.mdc`).
- One `styled.ts` per component folder; a shared hub `styled.ts` only when styles are
  genuinely shared.
- Web variants: `styled.web.ts` mirroring the native exports.

## No monoliths

- Hub files (navigators, registries) **wire and register only** — one screen/component body
  per subfolder.
- Split immediately when: multiple exported screens accumulate in one file, styles appear in
  a `.tsx`, or logic belongs in a `*Rules.ts`.

## Canonical leaf layout

```
<ScreenName>/
├── <ScreenName>.tsx
├── styled.ts
├── <screenName>Rules.ts
├── <screenName>Rules.test.ts
├── types.ts          # optional
└── index.ts
```

## Imports

- Folders expose a barrel `index.ts`; consumers import the folder, not deep paths.
- **Inside** a package: relative imports between siblings — never the package's own alias
  barrel (circular-dependency risk).
- **Across** features: path aliases — never `../../../` climbs.
